-- postpartum week 146: Copper and other trace minerals you don't hear as much about
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   146,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Copper and other trace minerals you don''t hear as much about',
   '"Copper, manganese and selenium are needed only in small amounts, and a varied everyday plate — nuts, seeds, whole grains, legumes, vegetables — already covers them for most people; this is about noticing that, not adding a new thing to track."',
   'This series has already covered several nutrients in their own dedicated weeks — iron, zinc, iodine, magnesium, potassium, vitamin B6, and selenium among them. A few trace minerals, copper especially, rarely get their own mention simply because true deficiency is uncommon on a reasonably varied diet. That''s worth knowing explicitly, rather than leaving it unmentioned and unexplained. The point of this week isn''t to add another thing to track. It''s reassurance, backed by specifics: the same everyday foods already recommended elsewhere in this series — nuts, seeds, whole grains, legumes, vegetables — cover copper, manganese and selenium for most people without any separate plan.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: keep following your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing going forward — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements — check in with your pharmacist if anything about that routine changes. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Maithil/Mithila home cooking (saagak jhor, tarua, machh-bhat, sanna, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This series has covered several individual nutrients by now — iron, zinc, iodine, magnesium among them. A few others get far less attention simply because deficiency is uncommon when the diet is reasonably varied. Copper is the main one this week: it works alongside iron to help your body actually use the iron you take in, and supports connective tissue and nerve function.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Saagak jhor, leafy greens in a thin gravy (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Maithil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saagak jhor", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Where copper actually comes from", "notes": "Cashews and other nuts, seeds (including sesame), whole grains, legumes, and organ meats are the main everyday sources of copper. You don''t need to eat all of these daily — a reasonably varied plate across the week is enough for most people, and there''s no need to track copper the way you might track iron or calcium.", "breakfast_a": "Tilkor tarua, ivy gourd leaf fritters in a rice batter (4-5 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a bhajia, mixed vegetable fritters (4-5 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Machh-bhat, Maithil-style fish curry with steamed rice (100g fish, 1 cup cooked rice) and a bhajia", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with a stuffed paratha (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Manganese — a brief mention, same idea", "notes": "Manganese works alongside several enzymes involved in metabolism and bone formation, and like copper it''s needed in only small amounts. Whole grains, legumes, nuts and leafy greens are the main sources — largely the same everyday foods that cover copper, so there''s no separate food list to remember here.", "breakfast_a": "Chana dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a thin rice-and-lentil crepe with a fried egg folded in (1 piece, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thadia saagak teeman, red spinach and lentils (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Maithil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Selenium — a quick cross-reference, not a repeat", "notes": "Selenium got its own full week earlier in this series (Week 115) — the short version stays the same: your thyroid needs it in only small amounts, and Brazil nuts, fish, eggs and grains cover it easily, with no benefit to taking more than needed. Brazil nuts are worth a specific mention on their own: their selenium content is unusually high and variable nut to nut, so treating them as an unrestricted daily handful — or combining them casually with a selenium supplement — can push intake too high in a way most other food sources don''t. If it''s useful to you, that earlier week goes into more detail than there''s room for here.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Arikanchanak tarkari, sun-dried colocasia leaves in a mustard gravy (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Maithil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an arikanchanak tarkari", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Missi roti, a spiced gram-flour flatbread (3 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with a missi roti (3 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "One caution — avoiding excessive intake, especially from supplements", "notes": "Copper, manganese and selenium can all become harmful in excess. From ordinary food that''s generally hard to do — Brazil nuts, covered on Day 4, are the notable exception worth remembering. Otherwise, this is mainly a caution about avoiding excessive intake from standalone supplements: don''t add a copper, manganese or selenium supplement on your own without a specific reason and your doctor''s guidance, since a typical varied diet already meets these needs for most people. Worth knowing: If you''re taking a multivitamin or mineral supplement already, there''s usually no need to add a separate copper, manganese or selenium supplement on top of it — check with your doctor before combining supplements.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna with jaggery (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Machh-bhat, Maithil-style fish curry with steamed rice (100g fish, 1 cup cooked rice) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The everyday version of all this", "notes": "None of this week''s minerals need their own tracking sheet. The practical takeaway is the same one that''s run through several of this series'' nutrient weeks: nuts, seeds, whole grains, legumes and a varied mix of vegetables across the week, alongside your usual protein and calcium sources, covers copper, manganese and selenium for most people without any special effort.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Daail-jhimni, fried ribbed gourd with lentils (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Maithil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Looking back at this week, does your usual plate already include nuts, seeds, whole grains and legumes fairly often — or is that a gap worth quietly closing?", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Saagak jhor, leafy greens in a thin gravy (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Maithil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a saagak jhor", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a food from this week''s list — nuts, seeds, whole grains — that you''d enjoy having more often, just because you like it?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 5 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, well beyond the first year.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 147: Eating well through batch cooking and meal prep
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   147,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well through batch cooking and meal prep',
   '"A few dishes cooked once and stored safely can carry you through several genuinely nourishing days — the win is in the safe cooling, storing and reheating, as much as the cooking itself."',
   'Cooking once and eating from it over several days is one of the most practical ways to keep eating well on genuinely busy weeks — but it comes with its own set of food-safety steps that matter as much as the recipe itself. This week is about both halves: what actually holds up well to batch cooking, freezing and reheating, and the cooling/storage/reheating practice that keeps it safe — not just convenient.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: keep following your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing going forward — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements — check in with your pharmacist if anything about that routine changes. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Old Delhi home cooking (akbari daal, korma, kebabs, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Cooking in larger batches and eating from them across the week can be a real help on busy days — but it only stays a good option if the food is cooled, stored and reheated safely. This week looks at both sides: what''s worth batch-cooking, and how to do it more safely.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Akbari daal, slow-cooked urad and chana dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken korma (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What batch-cooks well", "notes": "Dals, bean curries (rajma, chana), most gravies and kormas, and khichdi all freeze and reheat well, generally holding their quality for about three months in the freezer if stored properly. Rice needs the most care of anything here: cool it quickly, refrigerate promptly, and use it within 24 hours, or freeze it promptly instead — more on that below. A big pot of a dal or curry made once, then portioned, is often the most efficient batch-cooking choice.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, savoury gram-flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a thin rice-and-lentil crepe with a fried egg folded in (1 piece, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Shami kebab, minced chicken-and-lentil patties (3 pieces) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Cooling food safely before storing it", "notes": "The safety of batch cooking depends heavily on this cooling step — it''s the part that''s easiest to skip when you''re tired or busy, and the part that matters most. Smaller containers, refrigerated promptly, is the simple rule to keep in mind. Worth knowing: Cooked food left at room temperature for more than about two hours (one hour above roughly 32°C) moves into the range where bacteria multiply fastest. Divide a large batch into smaller, shallower containers so it cools faster, and refrigerate or freeze it within that window rather than leaving the whole pot out to cool completely first.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Akbari daal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chicken korma side (75g chicken)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Reheating properly, and the once-only rule", "notes": "Reheat stored food until it''s steaming hot all the way through, not just warm — a microwave can heat unevenly, so stir partway through and check a few spots. Only reheat a portion once: reheat what you''ll actually eat that sitting, rather than reheating the whole batch repeatedly and cooling it again each time, which raises the risk with every extra cycle.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Old Delhi-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Missi roti, a spiced gram-flour flatbread (3 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken korma (100g chicken) with a missi roti (3 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What''s better cooked fresh", "notes": "Not everything is worth batch-cooking. Fried or crisp items (pakoras, crisp kebabs), delicate vegetables like leafy greens, and freshly made rotis all lose texture badly on reheating — these are usually worth making closer to when you''ll eat them, even on a batch-cooking day, rather than storing and reheating. A simple middle path: batch-cook the dal or curry base, and make the bread or a fresh vegetable side on the day.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a fresh cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken korma (100g chicken) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a fresh cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a fresh spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Shami kebab, minced chicken-and-lentil patties (3 pieces) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a fresh spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Labelling and rotating — the practical habit that actually saves you", "notes": "The most useful habit isn''t a recipe at all — it''s labelling containers with the date before freezing, and eating the oldest ones first. A freezer with unlabelled containers from unknown weeks doesn''t actually save you time later, since it''s harder to trust what''s in it. A rough guide, assuming your refrigerator runs at 4°C or below and your freezer at -18°C or below: cooked dals and curries keep about 3-4 days refrigerated or up to about 3 months frozen; cooked rice is cooled quickly, refrigerated promptly, and used within 24 hours. Thaw frozen portions in the refrigerator, or using a microwave''s defrost setting, rather than leaving them out on the counter, and reheat straight after thawing.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Akbari daal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-vegetable pulao, spiced rice with kidney beans and vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Old Delhi-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or, fittingly, a safely stored portion from this week''s batch that''s still within its storage window, or a portion you froze earlier. If you tried batch cooking this week, did the cooling and storing steps fit into your routine, or is there a smaller version of this worth trying instead?", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Akbari daal, slow-cooked urad and chana dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken korma (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there one dish from this week you''d want to keep a regular batch of in your freezer, just because it makes a busy day easier?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 5 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, well beyond the first year.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 148: Eating well when you're the one hosting or cooking for a crowd
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   148,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re the one hosting or cooking for a crowd',
   '"Serving everyone else well doesn''t have to mean eating last, standing, or living off scraps from the kitchen — your own plate deserves the same planning as the guest list."',
   'Hosting or cooking for a group — family visiting, a celebration, a larger household gathering — often means everyone else eats well while the person who made it all happen eats standing up, eats last, or barely eats at all. That pattern is common enough to be worth naming directly, along with a few practical ways around it. None of this is about doing less for your guests. It''s about making sure a proper meal for yourself is part of the plan for the day, not an afterthought that depends on there being anything left over.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: keep following your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing going forward — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements — check in with your pharmacist if anything about that routine changes. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Lakshadweep home cooking (mas min/tuna curry, coconut rice, fish cutlets, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re the one hosting or cooking for a gathering — family visiting, a get-together, a larger household meal — it''s easy for your own eating to become an afterthought: a taste here, a bite there, and then a proper meal only once everyone else is done and the kitchen is finally quiet. This week is about protecting your own meal even while you''re managing everyone else''s.", "breakfast_a": "Coconut rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Light vegetable stew (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a light vegetable stew", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji, scrambled paneer with vegetables (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Set your own plate first, before you start serving", "notes": "A genuinely useful habit for a hosting day: serve your own plate at the same time as the first guest''s, before the serving and re-serving begins, and set it aside if you need to keep moving. It''s far easier to eat a proper meal in a pause than to try to reconstruct one later from whatever''s left in the pots. If you can''t get to it soon, refrigerate your plated portion promptly or keep it safely hot rather than leaving it out on the counter — discard perishable food left out longer than about two hours (one hour above roughly 32°C), since reheating it afterward doesn''t make food that sat out too long safe again.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated coconut (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mas min, Lakshadweep-style tuna curry made with skipjack (light) tuna, an FDA/EPA \"Best Choices\" fish (100g) with coconut rice (1 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "\"Tasting as you cook\" isn''t the same as eating", "notes": "A long cooking session for a crowd often means dozens of small tastes over several hours, which can feel like eating without actually covering what a full meal would. It''s worth noticing that distinction on a big cooking day specifically, so a real meal doesn''t quietly get skipped. Worth knowing: It''s common to assume all the tasting spoons through a long cooking session add up to a proper meal — they rarely do, and often skip protein and calcium entirely. Treat tasting as tasting, and still plan an actual sit-down portion for yourself.", "breakfast_a": "Chana dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a thin rice-and-lentil crepe with a fried egg folded in (1 piece, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Asking for a hand, without it being a whole conversation", "notes": "You don''t need a big discussion to get a little support on a hosting day — a simple, specific ask (\"can you plate this for me while I sit for ten minutes\") is often easier for people to act on than a general one. Even ten uninterrupted minutes to eat sitting down is a real difference from eating standing at the counter.", "breakfast_a": "Coconut rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Missi roti, a spiced gram-flour flatbread (3 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with a missi roti (3 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping your own food safe on a big-cooking day", "notes": "It''s easy to lose track of how long the serving dishes have been out when you''re focused on guests — worth a quick check for your own plate too, especially anything with meat, fish, egg or dairy in it. Worth knowing: On a day with a lot of food sitting out for guests over several hours, the same cooling and holding rules apply to your own plate as to the serving dishes: don''t eat from a dish that''s been sitting at room temperature for more than about two hours (one hour above roughly 32°C), and reheat anything you set aside for yourself until it''s steaming hot. Reheating doesn''t undo the risk from food that was already left out too long — when in doubt about the timing, it''s safer to discard it.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a light vegetable stew", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Mas min, Lakshadweep-style tuna curry made with skipjack (light) tuna, an FDA/EPA \"Best Choices\" fish (100g) with coconut rice (1 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The day after — restocking yourself, not just the fridge", "notes": "After a big hosting day, it''s common to focus entirely on clearing up and restocking the kitchen. Worth giving your own meals the same attention the next day — a properly sized breakfast and lunch, rather than running on leftovers snatched between chores, helps you actually recover from a demanding day rather than just push through it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Next time you''re hosting, is there one small change from this week — plating your own food first, asking for ten minutes to sit — worth trying?", "breakfast_a": "Coconut rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Light vegetable stew (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a light vegetable stew", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji, scrambled paneer with vegetables (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there someone in your life who''d genuinely be glad to help you eat properly on a hosting day, if you simply asked?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 5 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, well beyond the first year.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 149: Keeping your own eating steady when someone else in the family is unwell
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   149,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Keeping your own eating steady when someone else in the family is unwell',
   '"Caring well for someone who''s sick doesn''t require running your own meals into the ground — a few simple, low-effort options kept ready for exactly these days protect both of you."',
   'If someone else in your household — a partner, another child, an elder — is unwell right now, this week''s theme applies directly. If not, keep it in mind for whenever that stretch does come up; the general habits here also work well for any period where your own routine gets pushed aside by someone else''s needs. Caring for a sick family member is real, necessary work, and it''s common for a caregiver''s own meals to be the first thing that slips — skipped, eaten cold, or replaced by whatever''s fastest. This week is about a few low-effort ways to keep your own eating reasonably steady through exactly that kind of stretch, without adding to what''s already a demanding week.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: an irregular routine can affect glucose management — follow your own plan''s guidance on portions and timing, and contact your care team promptly if you can''t manage your intake or glucose within it, rather than waiting to see if a hectic stretch passes. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements — check in with your pharmacist if a disrupted routine makes that harder to keep to some days. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Santhal Adivasi home cooking (dhuska, dal pitha, muri ghuguni, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "When a partner, another child, or an elder in your household is unwell, it''s natural for your attention — and your own meals — to slide down the priority list. Appointments, medicines, extra washing and disturbed sleep can all eat into the time you''d normally spend on your own food. This week is about a few simple ways to keep your own eating reasonably steady through exactly that kind of stretch.", "breakfast_a": "Muri ghuguni, puffed rice with a spiced black chickpea curry (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Keep a small stock of foods that need almost no attention", "notes": "On a caregiving-heavy day, meals that require standing at the stove aren''t always realistic. Worth keeping a small stock of things that need very little preparation — curd, boiled eggs made in a batch, roasted nuts, fruit, or a dal cooked earlier in the week — so a proper meal doesn''t depend on having free time you don''t currently have. A genuine no-stove fallback for the busiest days, if today''s menu isn''t realistic: a small bowl of curd (150g, needs refrigeration) with roasted peanuts (15g, shelf-stable, no fridge needed) and a banana or other fruit (shelf-stable) — or 2 boiled eggs made in a batch earlier (refrigerate, use within a few days) with a slice of bread (shelf-stable) and a glass of milk (200ml, needs refrigeration). Either combination covers protein and calcium without any stove time on the day itself.", "breakfast_a": "Dhuska, a rice-and-lentil fried disc (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Santhal-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Eating in shorter windows is still eating", "notes": "If sitting down for a full meal isn''t happening right now, several smaller eating moments across the day still add up to real nourishment — they don''t need to look like a traditional three-meal pattern to count. The aim on a stretched day is getting enough food in total, not matching your usual mealtime structure exactly.", "breakfast_a": "Dal pitha, steamed rice-flour dumplings with a chana dal filling (4-5 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Letting someone else''s care extend to you too", "notes": "If a friend, neighbour or relative offers to help while you''re focused on someone else''s illness, a meal for you is a genuinely practical thing to say yes to — as useful as help with errands or childcare, even though it can feel like an odd thing to ask for on someone else''s behalf. It doesn''t have to be a big ask; even a single cooked dish shared over is real support.", "breakfast_a": "Chana dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Santhal-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Watching your own signs too, not only theirs", "notes": "It''s easy to become entirely focused on the unwell family member''s symptoms and appointments and lose track of your own wellbeing in the process. Checking in with yourself — sleep, appetite, mood — a couple of times through a longer stretch of caregiving is worth doing deliberately, since it''s not something that tends to happen on its own. Worth knowing: Prolonged disturbed sleep, skipped meals and high stress can affect you as well as whoever you''re caring for — persistent exhaustion, a marked drop in your own appetite over several days, or feeling unable to cope are worth mentioning to your own doctor, not just pushed through silently because someone else''s needs feel more urgent right now.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, savoury gram-flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a thin rice-and-lentil crepe with a fried egg folded in (1 piece, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Once things settle, rebuilding your own routine gently", "notes": "As the immediate caregiving intensity eases — the person recovers, appointments become less frequent — it''s a good moment to rebuild your own eating routine deliberately rather than assuming it''ll snap back on its own. A few consistent meals across a few days may help you regain some routine — and if your appetite, energy or mood still doesn''t feel right once things have settled, that''s still worth mentioning to your own doctor rather than waiting it out further.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Missi roti, a spiced gram-flour flatbread (3 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. If this stretch is still ongoing, is there one small thing from this week — a stocked shelf, a single ask for help — worth putting in place today?", "breakfast_a": "Muri ghuguni, puffed rice with a spiced black chickpea curry (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Looking after someone else well doesn''t mean looking after yourself less — is there one meal this week that felt like it was genuinely for you?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 5 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, well beyond the first year.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 150: Eating well through a move or a big change in your surroundings
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   150,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well through a move or a big change in your surroundings',
   '"An unfamiliar kitchen and an unpacked pantry are temporary — a few simple, low-equipment meals can carry you through the unsettled stretch until your routine catches up with your new surroundings."',
   'If you''re currently moving house, relocating, or otherwise going through a big change to your everyday surroundings, this week''s theme applies directly. If not, keep it in mind for whenever that stretch does come up — the same low-equipment, gradually-rebuilding approach works for any period where your usual kitchen and routine aren''t available to you. A move disrupts far more than furniture — it can disrupt where your food comes from, what you''re able to cook, and how settled you feel doing it. This week is about a realistic, temporary way through that, not a demand to have everything running smoothly from the first day in a new place.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: an irregular routine can affect glucose management — follow your own plan''s guidance on portions and timing, and contact your care team promptly if you can''t manage your intake or glucose within it during an unsettled stretch. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements even while things are unsettled — check in with your pharmacist if a house move disrupts your usual supply or routine. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Puducherry/Franco-Tamil home cooking (meen kuzhambu, mor kuzhambu, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A house move, a relocation, or any big change to your everyday surroundings can genuinely disrupt eating for a while — packed-up cookware, an unfamiliar kitchen, an unfamiliar local market, and simply not knowing where anything is yet. This week is about keeping yourself fed reasonably well through that in-between stretch, not about having it perfectly figured out from day one.", "breakfast_a": "Puducherry idli, slightly sweet idlis (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice (1 cup) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Mutton sambar, a mutton-and-lentil curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Before the kitchen is unpacked — a short list of no-cook basics", "notes": "In the first few days of a move, cooking might not be possible at all. Bread, peanut butter, roasted nuts and fruit don''t need refrigeration and can be kept ready without a working fridge — genuinely no-cook. Curd, milk and boiled eggs are different: they need refrigeration once opened or cooked, and boiled eggs need at least a kettle or a brief stove/hot-plate borrow, so they''re minimal-equipment rather than strictly no-cook. If your fridge isn''t set up yet, buy dairy in small amounts you''ll use the same day, or choose a shelf-stable UHT/tetra-pack milk, which is safe unrefrigerated until opened.", "breakfast_a": "Curd (150g) with a banana and roasted peanuts (15g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs, needs only a kettle or a brief stove) with bread (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bread (2 slices) with peanut butter (2 tbsp) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs, needs only a kettle or a brief stove) with bread (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Bread (2 slices) with peanut butter (2 tbsp) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs, needs only a kettle or a brief stove) with bread (2 slices) and roasted peanuts (15g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Finding your new local sources, gradually", "notes": "Locating a reliable dairy source, a usual grocery or vegetable seller, and a pharmacy in a new area doesn''t need to happen all at once — prioritising a dependable milk or curd source early on is worth it, since it anchors the calcium habit this series keeps coming back to, even while the rest of your routine is still settling.", "breakfast_a": "Appam, fermented rice pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Eating out more than usual, for now", "notes": "It''s common to eat out or order in more than usual around a move, simply because cooking isn''t set up yet. That''s a reasonable, temporary adjustment rather than something to feel bad about — where possible, choose options with a clear protein source (dal, egg, paneer, meat or fish) and a vegetable side, rather than only bread or rice-based dishes, to keep the week''s usual balance roughly on track.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a thin rice-and-lentil crepe with a fried egg folded in (1 piece, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton sambar, a mutton-and-lentil curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Food safety in an unfamiliar kitchen", "notes": "An unfamiliar stove, fridge or water source is worth a bit of extra attention in the first days, purely from a food-safety standpoint — the same care you''d already give your usual kitchen, just not yet on autopilot in a new one. Worth knowing: A borrowed, rented or brand-new kitchen may have appliances you haven''t used before — check a fridge''s actual temperature rather than assuming it''s cold enough, and be a little more cautious with anything that needs proper refrigeration until you''re confident it''s working as expected.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, savoury gram-flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mor kuzhambu, a spiced buttermilk-and-coconut curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mor kuzhambu side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Meen kuzhambu, tamarind-and-coconut fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Rebuilding a routine, once the essentials are unpacked", "notes": "Once the kitchen basics are unpacked and working, rebuilding a simple eating routine — even just a dependable breakfast and a planned dinner — tends to settle the rest of the day too. It doesn''t need to be your old routine exactly; a new one that fits your new surroundings is just as valid.", "breakfast_a": "Parotta, a flaky layered flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice (1 cup) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. If a move is still ahead of you or still settling, is there one no-cook basic from this week worth keeping on hand for the first few days?", "breakfast_a": "Puducherry idli, slightly sweet idlis (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice (1 cup) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindail, tangy slow-cooked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Mutton sambar, a mutton-and-lentil curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there something about a new kitchen or a new neighbourhood you''re genuinely looking forward to, once the unsettled part has passed?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 5 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, well beyond the first year.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 151: Eating well while you're supporting aging parents or in-laws
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   151,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well while you''re supporting aging parents or in-laws',
   '"Supporting a parent or in-law through aging is often a long, low-grade strain rather than one hard week — and a meal skipped in the middle of a phone call or a visit adds up the same way over months as it would over a single hard stretch."',
   'If you''re currently supporting a parent or in-law through age-related health issues, mobility changes or memory concerns — whether you''re nearby and hands-on or coordinating from a distance — this week''s theme applies directly. If not, it''s still worth keeping in mind, since this kind of caregiving tends to arrive gradually rather than announce itself. Unlike a short, acute stretch of caring for someone who''s unwell, supporting aging parents is often open-ended — it can stretch across years, easing and intensifying in turns. That makes it especially easy for your own meals to become a low, quiet casualty over time rather than an obvious one.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: an unpredictable caregiving schedule can make regular meal timing harder — follow your own care team''s guidance on portions and timing, and contact them if intake or glucose control is slipping over an extended stretch. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements even during a demanding caregiving stretch — a pharmacist can help if your own routine keeps slipping. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Bodo (Assam plains, Boro/Bodoland) home cooking (ondla jwng, narzi, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Caring about an aging parent or in-law — the phone calls, the appointments you help arrange, the visits, the worry that sits in the background — doesn''t always look like a crisis. It''s often a steady, ongoing presence in your life, which is exactly why it''s easy for your own meals to quietly slip for months without you noticing. Worth knowing: Fresh bamboo shoot contains natural compounds that need to be broken down before eating — use bamboo shoot that''s been properly boiled (changing the water once or twice) or the more common fermented/dried variety, rather than raw shoot straight from the plant.", "breakfast_a": "Ondla jwng, a Bodo rice-powder-and-vegetable curry (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bodo-style bamboo shoot chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Narzi-style dry fish curry with herbs, made with rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Keeping a call or a visit from displacing your own meal entirely", "notes": "A parent''s call or a visit to check on them can land at exactly the wrong moment for your own lunch, and it''s easy to just skip it rather than pick it back up afterward. Keeping something simple already made — a dal from earlier in the week, boiled eggs, curd and fruit — means there''s still a real meal waiting when the call ends, rather than nothing until the next one.", "breakfast_a": "Til pitha, sesame-and-rice flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rajma curry, a kidney bean preparation (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Worry isn''t a meal — and it doesn''t have to stop you from eating one", "notes": "Ongoing worry about a parent''s health, mobility or memory can sit with you at mealtimes without you consciously noticing it''s affecting your appetite. If you''ve realised you''re eating less than usual over weeks rather than days, that''s worth naming to yourself as its own thing, separate from whatever''s actually happening with them.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bodo-style bamboo shoot chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re the one coordinating care from a distance", "notes": "Arranging a parent or in-law''s care from another city — calls with doctors, managing help remotely, the guilt of not being physically there — carries its own particular exhaustion, distinct from hands-on caregiving but just as capable of crowding out your own routine. Distance doesn''t make the load lighter, so it''s worth extending the same basic meal-protecting habits to yourself here too.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Narzi-style dry fish curry with herbs, made with rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Accepting help with them frees up something for you too", "notes": "A sibling, a home-care aide, or another relative taking a shift of a parent''s care isn''t just practical for them — it''s also a genuine chance to reclaim a proper meal or a moment to yourself, if you let it be that rather than filling the freed-up time with something else you feel you should be doing instead.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, savoury gram-flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Soya chunk curry (100g soya chunks) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "This is likely a long stretch, not a short one — pace yourself accordingly", "notes": "Supporting aging parents or in-laws well often isn''t a few intense weeks that resolve — it can run for years, on and off. Treating your own eating as something to sustain steadily, rather than something to sacrifice now and fix later, tends to hold up much better across a stretch like that.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Is there one small, protected part of your day — even fifteen minutes — that could reliably stay yours, regardless of how a parent''s care is going that week?", "breakfast_a": "Ondla jwng, a Bodo rice-powder-and-vegetable curry (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bodo-style bamboo shoot chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Narzi-style dry fish curry with herbs, made with rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one thing you''d want a friend to remind you of, if she noticed you skipping meals while caring for someone else?',
   'Next week begins Phase 6 of 6''s second theme — everyday nourishment for you, heading toward the three-year mark and beyond.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 152: Eating well while you're actively trying to conceive again
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   152,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well while you''re actively trying to conceive again',
   '"There''s no single food or supplement that guarantees conception — steady, varied eating and a doctor''s guidance on any supplement matter far more than any one "fertility food" you''ll hear about from someone well-meaning."',
   'If you''re actively trying to conceive again right now, this week''s guidance is meant to be practical and reassuring rather than one more thing to worry about getting exactly right. If that''s not where you are, keep it in mind for whenever it does apply — the same steady approach holds. Trying to conceive can bring a flood of advice, much of it food-related and not all of it accurate. The aim here is a plain, evidence-based version: eat in a varied, adequate way, keep caffeine and alcohol sensible, and leave anything supplement-related to a conversation with your doctor rather than guesswork.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: well-managed blood sugar matters for conception and early pregnancy alike — follow your own care team''s guidance on portions, timing and any medication review before conceiving. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: PCOS can affect conception directly, and your care team may have specific guidance beyond general balanced eating — flexible, varied eating across the week still matters, but follow their specific recommendations for you. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: thyroid levels matter for conception and early pregnancy — keep following your prescribed medication timing and mention that you''re trying to conceive at your next thyroid check, since your dose may need reviewing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Gorkha/Nepali (Darjeeling hills) home cooking (dhindo, gundruk, sel roti, momo, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re actively trying to conceive again, you''ve probably already heard no shortage of food-related advice — some useful, plenty of it not. This week is a plain, steady version: what actually matters for your own eating right now, without the pressure to overhaul everything or chase a specific \"fertility food.\"", "breakfast_a": "Sel roti, a sweet fried rice-flour ring (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dhindo, a buckwheat-and-millet porridge (1 cup) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a gundruk sadeko side (fermented greens)", "lunch_b": "Chicken momo, steamed dumplings filled with spiced minced chicken (6 pieces) with a gundruk sadeko side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Gorkha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Caffeine and alcohol, while you''re trying", "notes": "Keep caffeine below about 200 mg a day. Because early pregnancy can be unrecognised for several weeks, avoiding alcohol while trying is the simplest cautious choice. This is not about panic or perfection; it is about a steady precaution.", "breakfast_a": "Chura sabzi, flattened rice with vegetables, ginger and garlic (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Trout curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Folate, briefly — you''ve covered this before", "notes": "This series looked at folate in depth back in Week 121 — while you''re actively trying to conceive, the guidance is more specific: food folate (leafy greens, lentils, citrus) is worth keeping up, but it isn''t considered a substitute for a folic acid supplement. The standard advice (CDC, ACOG) is 400 mcg of folic acid daily, ideally starting at least one month before conception and continuing through the first 12 weeks of pregnancy — a higher dose is sometimes advised for specific risk factors, but only on your clinician''s guidance, not on your own.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken momo, steamed dumplings filled with spiced minced chicken (6 pieces) with a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "No special \"fertility diet\" required", "notes": "There''s no scientifically established diet that reliably increases the odds of conceiving beyond generally eating well — steady, varied meals across the week matter more than any specific combination promoted online. If a relative or well-meaning friend suggests a specific food to \"help things along,\" it''s fine to nod and carry on with your usual balanced plate.", "breakfast_a": "Dhindo with grated vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Gorkha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Crash dieting now can work against you, not for you", "notes": "If you''re also hoping to be at a different weight before conceiving, a crash diet or sudden drastic restriction can disrupt your cycle rather than help it — steady, adequate eating alongside any weight goal your doctor has actually recommended tends to serve you better than an abrupt overhaul. This isn''t a suggestion that any particular weight is required to conceive.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kinema, a fermented soybean curry (100g soybean) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Darjeeling-style fish curry, made with rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If it''s taking longer than you hoped", "notes": "Trying for a while without a result can be genuinely disheartening, and it''s easy to respond by second-guessing every food choice as if diet alone explains it. If it''s been going on longer than feels right to you, that''s a conversation worth having directly with your doctor rather than a food problem to solve on your own.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Is there one piece of food-related advice you''ve been given about trying to conceive that''s actually been adding stress rather than helping?", "breakfast_a": "Sel roti, a sweet fried rice-flour ring (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dhindo, a buckwheat-and-millet porridge (1 cup) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a gundruk sadeko side (fermented greens)", "lunch_b": "Chicken momo, steamed dumplings filled with spiced minced chicken (6 pieces) with a gundruk sadeko side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Gorkha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to eat well this stretch without treating every meal as part of a fertility project?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 6 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, heading toward the three-year mark and beyond.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 153: Eating well as eating out and restaurant meals become a regular part of life again
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   153,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well as eating out and restaurant meals become a regular part of life again',
   '"A restaurant meal isn''t a lapse to correct for — as your child gets older and going out becomes routine again, eating well is about steady choices across the week, not treating one dinner out as something to make up for."',
   'If eating out is becoming a regular part of life again for your family, this week is meant to make that feel easy rather than fraught. If it''s not yet where you are, keep it in mind for when it is. There''s nothing inherently at odds between eating well and eating out. The goal this week is a practical, guilt-free approach: enjoy the meal, use a little ordinary judgement about food safety and portions, and let the rest of the week continue as usual.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: restaurant portions of rice, bread or dessert can be larger or richer than home portions — asking for a smaller portion or sharing a dish are both reasonable options, and this is worth discussing with your care team if eating out becomes frequent. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: the same general approach applies — a restaurant meal doesn''t need to be treated differently from any other meal; follow your own care team''s broader guidance for your day-to-day eating. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing regardless of where a meal is eaten — a restaurant breakfast doesn''t change the fasting or spacing guidance your prescriber gave you. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Khandeshi (North Maharashtra) home cooking (bhakari, varan, vafole, shev bhaji, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "As your child gets a little older, eating out or ordering in probably starts happening more often again — a birthday lunch, a weekend outing, a takeaway on a tired evening. None of that needs guilt attached. This week is about enjoying it sensibly, not avoiding it.", "breakfast_a": "Bhakari, a millet flatbread (2 pieces) with varan, a simple toor dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vafole, steamed lentil-and-vegetable dumplings (1 cup) with a kadhi (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Khandeshi-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with bhakari (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A rough plan makes restaurant meals easier, not stricter", "notes": "You don''t need to pre-decide exactly what you''ll order — a loose plan (roughly what kind of meal, roughly how it fits into the rest of the day) tends to make eating out feel easy rather than like a decision under pressure once you''re seated and hungry.", "breakfast_a": "Shev bhaji, a spiced curry with crunchy gram-flour noodles (1 cup) with bhakari (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pomfret curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Eating out doesn''t cancel out the rest of the week", "notes": "One richer restaurant meal doesn''t undo a week of otherwise steady eating, and it doesn''t need a stricter meal afterward to compensate either — the next meal can just be another ordinary one.", "breakfast_a": "Muthiya, steamed spiced millet-flour dumplings (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Usal, a sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Toor dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A few practical things to watch for when eating out", "notes": "A few small habits help when eating out becomes routine: checking that meat and fish look and taste fully cooked, being cautious with buffet items that may have sat out a while, and asking about ingredients if you''re managing a condition like diabetes or a food allergy. None of this should stop you from enjoying the meal — it''s just a bit of ordinary care.", "breakfast_a": "Bhakari (2 pieces) with a mixed vegetable sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Khandeshi-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka-style curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Let the restaurant meal be a meal, not an errand", "notes": "On a family outing, it''s easy to organise everyone else and barely eat yourself. Choose something you genuinely enjoy with a clear protein source and, where practical, a vegetable dish. Eat enough to feel satisfied; there is no need to compensate later.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If you''re managing a condition and eating out feels harder", "notes": "If you''re managing diabetes, PCOS or another condition, eating out can feel like it needs more planning than it''s worth — it doesn''t have to. Most restaurants can adjust rice or bread portions or tell you how a dish is prepared if you ask, and a single meal out is not a setback to a well-managed condition.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a rice-and-lentil crepe topped with a spiced egg layer (1 dosa) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Tandoori-style chicken (100g chicken) with a roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. What''s one restaurant or dish you''ve been looking forward to going back to as things settle into a new normal?", "breakfast_a": "Bhakari, a millet flatbread (2 pieces) with varan, a simple toor dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vafole, steamed lentil-and-vegetable dumplings (1 cup) with a kadhi (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Khandeshi-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with bhakari (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a small worry about eating out you''re carrying that isn''t really necessary anymore?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 6 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, heading toward the three-year mark and beyond.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 154: Eating well while you manage a long-term injury or a minor surgery recovery unrelated to childbirth
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   154,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well while you manage a long-term injury or a minor surgery recovery unrelated to childbirth',
   '"Healing from an injury or a minor procedure takes real building blocks — protein, and enough food overall — not a single miracle ingredient; steady, adequate eating supports recovery more than any one food ever could."',
   'If you''re recovering from an injury or a minor procedure right now, this week is meant to give your eating a bit of extra intention without turning it into a project. If this isn''t where you are, keep it in mind for whenever it applies. Recovery, whatever its cause, draws on the same basics: adequate protein, adequate overall food, and a handful of specific nutrients involved in tissue repair. None of this requires a special diet — just a bit more attention to the fundamentals you''re already practising.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: reduced activity during recovery can affect blood sugar patterns — keep monitoring as usual and mention the injury or surgery to your care team if your readings shift. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: there''s no specific interaction between PCOS and injury recovery beyond general balanced eating — follow your own care team''s broader guidance alongside this week''s suggestions. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing through recovery, and mention any new medication (such as a pain reliever) to your prescriber in case of interactions. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team, and from whoever is overseeing your recovery, always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kayastha (Awadh, Uttar Pradesh) home cooking (shami kebab, dal keema, kathal kabab, bhuna zimikand, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re recovering from an injury or a minor surgery right now — unrelated to childbirth, whatever stage of motherhood you''re in — your body still needs steady building blocks to heal: enough protein, enough overall food, and a bit of extra rest where you can manage it.", "breakfast_a": "Bhuna zimikand, pan-roasted spiced yam (1 cup) with a moong dal (1/2 cup), a roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal keema-style moong dal with vegetables (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Shami kebab, spiced minced-meat patties (2 pieces, about 100g) with a roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Bhuna kathal, pan-roasted spiced jackfruit (1 cup) with a chana dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Kayastha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Protein matters more than usual right now", "notes": "Tissue repair — whether from a fracture, a sprain, or a minor surgical incision — draws on protein more than everyday maintenance does. Including a protein source at each main meal (dal, paneer, eggs, meat or fish, depending on what you eat) is a sensible, practical habit while you''re healing, without needing to calculate anything precisely.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with peanuts and vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pomfret curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If your activity has dropped while you heal", "notes": "If an injury has temporarily reduced how active you are, your appetite may naturally shift too — that''s fine and expected. It''s still worth keeping protein and overall food intake steady rather than cutting back sharply, since your body is doing repair work even while you''re resting more than usual.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Lobia curry, a black-eyed pea preparation (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled pomfret (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Vitamin C and zinc, briefly", "notes": "Vitamin C (citrus, guava, amla, bell peppers) and zinc (legumes, nuts, seeds, meat, dairy) both play a role in normal wound healing — a varied diet across the week typically covers both without needing a separate supplement, though your surgeon or doctor may have specific advice if you''ve had a procedure.", "breakfast_a": "Dal keema-style chana dal with vegetables (3/4 cup) with a roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Shami kebab, spiced minced-meat patties (2 pieces, about 100g) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Kayastha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If cooking itself is harder right now", "notes": "If the injury makes standing, chopping or cooking harder than usual, this is a good stretch to lean on simple one-pot meals, pre-cut vegetables, or help from family — a simpler meal made with less effort is still a good meal, and it''s not a compromise worth feeling bad about.", "breakfast_a": "Bhuna kathal, pan-roasted spiced jackfruit (1 cup) with a masoor dal (1/2 cup), a roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Recovery timelines vary, and that''s normal", "notes": "Some injuries and minor surgeries heal in weeks; others take months. There''s no single \"correct\" pace, and continuing to eat well throughout — rather than only in the first week or two — supports whatever timeline your body needs.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Grilled pomfret (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Is there a small kindness you could offer yourself this week while your body does its repair work?", "breakfast_a": "Bhuna zimikand, pan-roasted spiced yam (1 cup) with a moong dal (1/2 cup), a roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dal keema-style moong dal with vegetables (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Shami kebab, spiced minced-meat patties (2 pieces, about 100g) with a roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Bhuna kathal, pan-roasted spiced jackfruit (1 cup) with a chana dal (1/2 cup), rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Kayastha-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat this recovery with the same patience you''d want for anyone else going through it?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 6 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, heading toward the three-year mark and beyond.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
