-- postpartum week 155: Eating well as your child starts full-time school
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   155,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well as your child starts full-time school',
   '"A longer stretch of your day just opened up now that school has started — it''s worth spending a little of it on your own proper meal, not just errands and catching up on everything else."',
   'If your child has just started, or is about to start, full-time school, this week is meant for you — not a guide to packing lunchboxes or managing homework, but a nudge to notice the shift in your own daily rhythm and use a bit of it for yourself. This is a genuine milestone in your own life as a mother, not only your child''s — the structure of your days is changing, sometimes for the first time in years. Giving your own meals a bit of the space that opens up is a reasonable, ordinary way to mark that.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: a new daily schedule can shift your usual meal timing — keep monitoring as your routine settles, and adjust meal timing with your care team''s guidance if needed. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: a changing daily routine is a good moment to check whether your usual eating pattern still fits your schedule — flexible, varied eating across the week still matters most. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: if your morning routine has shifted, double check that your medication timing relative to food and other supplements still holds — ask your prescriber if the new schedule makes the usual timing difficult. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Bhil tribal (Rajasthan-Madhya Pradesh-Gujarat belt) home cooking (maize roti, paniya, rabdi, thuli, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Full-time school is a real shift in your own daily rhythm, not just your child''s — mornings look different, and there''s a longer stretch of the day that isn''t built around them in quite the same way. This week is about noticing that shift and using a little of it for your own steady eating.", "breakfast_a": "Maize roti, a maize flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Paniya, steamed maize-and-lentil dumplings (1 cup) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Bhil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with maize roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The morning rush is its own kind of chaos", "notes": "New school-morning routines can be genuinely hectic, and it''s easy to let your own breakfast slide in the scramble to get everyone out the door. A breakfast you can eat quickly, even standing at the counter, still counts as a real meal.", "breakfast_a": "Thuli, a maize-flour porridge with milk (1 cup)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rohu curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The quiet house can feel strange at first", "notes": "The first stretch of a genuinely quiet house during school hours can feel oddly unsettling after years of a child nearby — that''s a normal adjustment, not something wrong with you. It''s also, practically, a good window for a proper, unhurried meal of your own.", "breakfast_a": "Rabdi, a slow-simmered thickened-milk dish (1 cup) with a piece of maize roti", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chana-and-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Packing a lunchbox doesn''t mean skipping your own lunch", "notes": "A quick note, since this often happens without noticing: putting real thought and effort into your child''s lunchbox is not the same as eating your own lunch, and the two can quietly get conflated on a busy morning. Your own meal still needs its own time, even a short one.", "breakfast_a": "Maize roti (2 pieces) with a mixed vegetable sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bhil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you''re returning to a full workday too", "notes": "For many mothers, a child starting full-time school lines up with returning to a fuller workday of their own — that''s two adjustments happening at once, and it''s fair to expect the transition to take a few weeks to settle, meals included.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A little more time can mean a little more for you", "notes": "As the new routine settles in over the next few weeks, some of the time freed up by school hours can reasonably go toward things that support you — cooking a meal you actually enjoy, sitting down properly to eat it, or simply resting. None of that needs to feel indulgent.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a rice-and-lentil crepe topped with a spiced egg layer (1 dosa) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Grilled rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. What''s one thing you''d like to do with the extra bit of time school hours have given you?", "breakfast_a": "Maize roti, a maize flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Paniya, steamed maize-and-lentil dumplings (1 cup) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Bhil-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with maize roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'How does it feel to notice this shift as a milestone in your own life, not just your child''s?',
   'Next week continues within Phase 6 of 6 — everyday nourishment for you, heading toward the three-year mark and beyond.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 156: Eating well when you're supporting a friend through her own new-motherhood stretch
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   156,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Eating well when you''re supporting a friend through her own new-motherhood stretch',
   '"You''re on the far side of the stretch a friend of yours might be right in the middle of now — you know from the inside what actually helps, and a meal you bring her is worth more than advice; just don''t let helping her come entirely at the cost of your own plate."',
   'If a friend of yours is in her own early postpartum stretch right now, you have something valuable to offer her that most people around her don''t: recent, lived memory of exactly what that''s like. This week looks at practical, food-related ways to support her — and at making sure supporting her doesn''t quietly come at the cost of your own meals. If this isn''t where you are right now, keep this week in your back pocket for whenever a friend needs it — the ideas here travel well beyond this exact week of the series.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: a busier week spent supporting a friend can disrupt your own meal timing — keep an eye on your usual pattern even on days that revolve around someone else''s needs. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: the same general approach applies here as any other week — flexible, varied eating across the week matters more than any single day going exactly to plan. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing even on days spent mostly at a friend''s place — carrying your morning dose with you if your schedule is uncertain is a reasonable habit. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on East Indian (the Mumbai Catholic community''s own home cooking — foogias, chitaps, apas, chicken tope, and similar), not the wider geographic East India. If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If a friend of yours has just had a baby, you''re in a good position to help in a way that actually matters — you remember, better than most people around her right now, how little bandwidth there is for cooking. A dropped-off meal, or simply sitting with her while she eats one, tends to help more than well-meant advice.", "breakfast_a": "Foogias, a light fried bread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Boiled chana with a coconut-and-vegetable stir-fry (1 cup) with jeera rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken tope, a slow-cooked East Indian chicken curry (100g chicken) with jeera rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "East Indian-style crab curry masala (100g crab, choosing a variety confirmed as a lower-mercury choice by applicable local guidance) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "You remember what actually helped you", "notes": "Think back to your own early weeks with this series — what genuinely helped wasn''t usually advice, it was someone bringing food, or simply doing the dishes without being asked. That''s worth passing on to your friend now, in whatever form fits her.", "breakfast_a": "Chitaps, a soft rice-and-coconut pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rajma curry, a kidney bean preparation (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Supporting her doesn''t mean skipping your own meal", "notes": "It''s easy, when you''re cooking an extra portion for a friend or spending an afternoon at her place, to let your own meal become an afterthought. Feed yourself properly too — you can''t keep showing up for her on an empty stomach for long.", "breakfast_a": "Apas, a soft fermented rice pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Coconut-and-chana stir-fry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Duck moile, a coconut-and-spice duck curry (100g duck) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What to say, and what not to", "notes": "The most useful things you can offer a new mother are often small and unglamorous: \"I''m dropping off dinner Tuesday, no need to talk\" beats \"let me know if you need anything.\" And a gentle reminder — you don''t need to relay every feeding or sleep opinion you''ve picked up along the way; she''ll ask if she wants it.", "breakfast_a": "Foogias, a light fried bread (2 pieces) with a mixed vegetable sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tope, a slow-cooked East Indian chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Sardine masala fry, pan-fried spiced sardines (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "You''re proof that it does get easier", "notes": "Something you can offer that no advice can: simply being there as living proof that the exhausting early stretch does ease up. You don''t need to say much — your friend seeing you a few years out, managing fine, can be quietly reassuring on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If it brings back memories of your own early weeks", "notes": "Spending time with a friend in the thick of new motherhood can stir up your own memories of that stretch, sometimes ones you hadn''t thought about in a while. That''s a normal response to a full-circle moment, not a sign anything is wrong — give yourself a little space for it too.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken frithad, a tangy coconut-and-vinegar chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. What''s one thing someone did for you in your own early weeks that you''d like to pass on to your friend now?", "breakfast_a": "Foogias, a light fried bread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Boiled chana with a coconut-and-vegetable stir-fry (1 cup) with jeera rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken tope, a slow-cooked East Indian chicken curry (100g chicken) with jeera rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "East Indian-style crab curry masala (100g crab, choosing a variety confirmed as a lower-mercury choice by applicable local guidance) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Looking back at how far you''ve come since your own Week 1 — what would you most want a friend just starting out to know?',
   'Next week closes out this three-year series — a chance to look back over everything this journey has covered.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
