-- postpartum week 157: Three years of Nourish — a bonus closing week
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   157,
   NULL,
   'Phase 6 of 6 — Three years and beyond',
   'Three years of Nourish — a bonus closing week',
   '"None of this was ever about a perfect plate — it was about showing up for your own eating, in whatever small, imperfect way you could, week after week, for three years. That''s the whole thing, and you did it."',
   NULL,
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: the carb-conscious habits this series has repeated — steady meal timing, mindful portions, checking in with your care team — are worth carrying forward well past this final week. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: the same flexible, varied approach to eating that''s run through this whole series remains a reasonable ongoing habit, alongside whatever specific guidance your care team has given you. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: the medication-timing habit this series has repeated every week — consistent timing relative to food and other supplements — is worth keeping as a permanent routine, not just a series-long one. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence, in this final week and every week after it.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This closing week deliberately doesn''t introduce a new regional cuisine — instead it returns to the plain, familiar home-style combinations (dal-rice-sabzi, khichdi, poha, upma, paratha) that have run through this whole series since Week 1, as a fitting way to close rather than open something new.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor — the arc of the whole journey", "notes": "This series began in the rawest, earliest days after birth — when a full glass of water and a plate you didn''t have to cook yourself felt like a lot. It moves, over three years, through finding a rhythm, rebuilding strength, settling into it, and a long stretch of ordinary life carrying on. This final week is simply a chance to look back at all of it before closing the book.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, flattened rice with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a dal of your choice (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Khichdi, a simple rice-and-lentil dish (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What you''ve learned about your own eating", "notes": "Somewhere across these three years, you likely learned things about your own eating that no single week could have taught you on its own — what steadies you, what a real \"enough\" portion looks like for your own body, and that skipping your own meal never actually served anyone else better. Whatever you''ve picked up, it''s yours to keep using long after this series ends.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rohu curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What you''d tell a mother just starting Week 1", "notes": "If a friend of yours were starting this exact series today, at her own Week 1, what would you want her to know? Perhaps that it gets easier, that no single missed meal undoes anything, or simply that she''s allowed to take care of herself too. Whatever comes to mind is worth holding onto — you''ve earned it.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, a savoury gram-flour pancake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rajma curry, a kidney bean preparation (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled rohu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Gratitude for whoever helped you get here", "notes": "Three years of steadier eating rarely happens entirely alone — a partner who took over cooking on hard days, a parent or in-law who dropped off a meal, a friend who simply asked how you were doing. If someone comes to mind, this is a good day to tell them so, even briefly.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What continues after this series ends", "notes": "This is the last week of Nourish, but nothing about eating well for yourself needs to end with it. The same basics that ran through all 157 weeks still hold: eat regularly, include protein and calcium sources you can rely on, drink enough water, and treat your own meal as worth planning for, not optional. You don''t need a new week''s worksheet to keep doing that.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A closing note", "notes": "Three years ago this series began with a mother who had just given birth, uncertain of a great many things. Whatever the road looked like in between, you kept showing up for yourself, one week at a time. That''s genuinely worth marking. Thank you for staying with it this far.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa, a rice-and-lentil crepe topped with a spiced egg layer (1 dosa) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chana masala, a spiced chickpea curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Curd rice, tempered rice mixed with curd (1 1/2 cups) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — the same restful, no-decisions pattern this series has offered every single week. Of everything this series has offered over three years, what''s the one habit you most want to keep?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, flattened rice with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a dal of your choice (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Home-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Khichdi, a simple rice-and-lentil dish (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus vegetables) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'If you could send one sentence back to yourself in Week 1, what would it be?',
   NULL)
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
